    "['set protocols pim family [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 pim', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 pim', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco IOS command for the Juniper `set protocols pim family inet6` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nipv6 pim\\n##\\n\\nThis command enables PIM (Protocol Independent Multicast) for IPv6 on a Cisco router. In Cisco IOS, unlike Juniper which separates IPv4 and IPv6 PIM with separate family statements, IPv6 PIM is enabled globally with this single command. However, note that you would still need to enable PIM on individual interfaces separately in Cisco (with `ipv6 pim sparse-mode` or similar interface-level commands).\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 pim'}]]": 1,
    "['set protocols pim interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco command enables PIM sparse mode for IPv6 on the specified interface, which is the equivalent functionality to Juniper's PIM interface configuration. Note that Cisco requires explicit enabling of sparse-mode (the most common PIM mode), while Juniper enables PIM by default when you add an interface to the PIM configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/0\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco command enables PIM sparse mode for IPv6 on the specified interface, which is the equivalent functionality to Juniper's PIM interface configuration. Note that Cisco requires explicit enabling of sparse-mode (the most common PIM mode), while Juniper enables PIM by default when you add an interface to the PIM configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode'}]]": 1,
    "['set protocols pim interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe translation:\\n1. The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s \"ge-1/0/1.0\" to Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet1/0/1\"\\n2. Juniper enables PIM by default when adding an interface under the PIM protocol hierarchy, while Cisco requires explicit \"ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" command\\n3. The \".0\" subunit notation is dropped as Cisco doesn\\'t use this numbering scheme in the same way', 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe translation:\\n1. The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s \"ge-1/0/1.0\" to Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet1/0/1\"\\n2. Juniper enables PIM by default when adding an interface under the PIM protocol hierarchy, while Cisco requires explicit \"ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" command\\n3. The \".0\" subunit notation is dropped as Cisco doesn\\'t use this numbering scheme in the same way', 'trans_template': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode'}]]": 1,
    "['set protocols pim interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/2\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe command enables PIM in sparse mode for IPv6 on the GigabitEthernet1/0/2 interface, which is Cisco's equivalent to Juniper's PIM interface configuration for IPv6. Note that in Cisco:\\n1. The interface naming convention is slightly different (GigabitEthernet instead of ge)\\n2. IPv6 PIM must be explicitly enabled per interface\\n3. We specify the mode (sparse-mode in this case) which is implied in Juniper's default configuration\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper PIM interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/2\\n ipv6 pim sparse-mode\\n##\\n\\nThe command enables PIM in sparse mode for IPv6 on the GigabitEthernet1/0/2 interface, which is Cisco's equivalent to Juniper's PIM interface configuration for IPv6. Note that in Cisco:\\n1. The interface naming convention is slightly different (GigabitEthernet instead of ge)\\n2. IPv6 PIM must be explicitly enabled per interface\\n3. We specify the mode (sparse-mode in this case) which is implied in Juniper's default configuration\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 pim sparse-mode'}]]": 1,
